1
00:00:00,950 --> 00:00:00,960
[Music]

2
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:07,909
This is software engineering radio, the podcast for professional developers on the web at se-radio.net.

3
00:00:07,919 --> 00:00:15,750
SE radio is brought to you by the ITE computer society and by it less software magazine online at computer.org/software.

4
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:19,189
[Music]

5
00:00:19,199 --> 00:00:32,069
Hi everyone, this is Priyanka Ragwin for software engineering radio and today we will discuss the topic views of AI for debugging and we look at three aspects of this show.

6
00:00:32,069 --> 00:00:35,510
One is going to be about using AI as an assistant to debug.

7
00:00:35,510 --> 00:00:44,950
Two AI debugging tools and three is it possible that an AI given a bug can help fix and do this autonomously.

8
00:00:44,950 --> 00:00:51,750
For this we have Mark Williamson as a guest and Mark is the CTO for UNDO.

9
00:00:51,750 --> 00:01:01,990
He's also a specialist in kernel level low-level Linux embedded development with a wide experience in crossd disciplinary engineering.

10
00:01:01,990 --> 00:01:04,070
He programs a lot in C and C++.

11
00:01:04,070 --> 00:01:12,231
And one of his proudest achievements from the Andu website is his quest towards an all green test suite. So Mark, welcome to the show.

12
00:01:12,231 --> 00:01:18,149
Is there anything else in your bio that you would like to add apart from what I've just introduced you as?

13
00:01:18,159 --> 00:01:20,310
I think that's a pretty good summary.

14
00:01:20,310 --> 00:01:29,030
I guess in my time at Undo, most of my last 11 years has been a quest to get people to appreciate debuggers more, and I'm glad to be here talking about them.

15
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:30,950
They're one of my favorite subjects.

16
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:33,749
Great.

17
00:01:33,749 --> 00:01:40,870
So, we'll kick off the show by asking you to define debugging in a professional software engineering context.

18
00:01:40,870 --> 00:01:42,789
and how does it differ from simply fixing bugs?

19
00:01:42,799 --> 00:01:44,789
Thanks.

20
00:01:44,789 --> 00:01:48,870
I really like this question because I think it's often misunderstood.

21
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:54,389
Developers actually spend most of that time at the computer debugging.

22
00:01:54,389 --> 00:01:59,670
It's easy to have a view that bug reports come in from the field from customers.

23
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:04,069
They go into a GitHub issue tracker or something like that.

24
00:02:04,069 --> 00:02:07,109
They get taken out and a developer fixes the bug.

25
00:02:07,109 --> 00:02:21,270
But I would say debugging is the quest to understand what your program is actually doing and why it's not what you expected and that starts the instant you've typed in your first code.

26
00:02:21,270 --> 00:02:26,390
So actually I would say that most of development is itself debugging.

27
00:02:26,390 --> 00:02:37,350
I've seen a lot of stats recently that only about 30% of developer work is programming and therefore coding agents aren't solving the whole end to end problem.

28
00:02:37,350 --> 00:02:46,070
But actually I would say that probably 80% of that 30% is debugging not typing in the code.

29
00:02:46,070 --> 00:02:48,949
Code generation is actually a very small part of what developers do.

30
00:02:48,959 --> 00:02:55,750
And a lot of the technical work is this debugging process of answering questions and gaining understanding.

31
00:02:55,760 --> 00:03:00,869
One of the things I wanted to ask you is what do you use debugging for?

32
00:03:00,869 --> 00:03:08,550
So if a program does not function as the way it is supposed to then that's called a runtime issue.

33
00:03:08,550 --> 00:03:11,350
Then that would be something that you would debug.

34
00:03:11,350 --> 00:03:16,229
But how about a case when it's not performing very well?

35
00:03:16,229 --> 00:03:19,270
Is that also a case where you would use debugging for?

36
00:03:19,270 --> 00:03:21,910
I would say yes.

37
00:03:21,910 --> 00:03:25,030
I think different developers might call this a different process,

38
00:03:25,030 --> 00:03:36,070
but I would say debugging is anytime you're trying to answer what happened or why did that happen and that includes performance issues.

39
00:03:36,070 --> 00:03:40,390
But you have to then broaden your understanding of what a debugging tool is.

40
00:03:40,390 --> 00:03:41,910
So I would say there's lots of tools you can use.

41
00:03:41,910 --> 00:03:43,990
You can add print fs into your code.

42
00:03:43,990 --> 00:03:46,869
They're often logging frameworks.

43
00:03:46,869 --> 00:03:53,350
There's also system level utilities like S trace, GDB,

44
00:03:53,350 --> 00:04:00,630
valrind, and Perf and actually even also version control in order to go back and figure out when a regression came in.

45
00:04:00,630 --> 00:04:02,390
I would say performance is in that continuum.

46
00:04:02,390 --> 00:04:08,869
So you might use a performance profiler, but actually why did the control flow bring you to the hot path?

47
00:04:08,869 --> 00:04:11,750
Well, that's maybe logging or a debugger.

48
00:04:11,750 --> 00:04:16,629
And it's also a question of using each tool's output to figure out what the next tool you apply should be.

49
00:04:16,639 --> 00:04:18,550
That's interesting.

50
00:04:18,550 --> 00:04:26,710
This brings me back to one of the episodes we did in SE radio which is 3544 which was on debugging

51
00:04:26,710 --> 00:04:40,950
and the host there had questioned the guest how debugging differs based on the languages paradigms whether you're debugging a monolith versus a micros service or just the way how you use the tools like as you said.

52
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:45,270
So in your experience maybe could you talk a little bit about that as well?

53
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:50,950
Yeah, there is a lot of variation I'd say.

54
00:04:50,950 --> 00:05:01,350
So what I've noticed in the field in my experience is there is a common denominator in everybody's debugging experience and that is putting more print statements in.

55
00:05:01,350 --> 00:05:05,590
It's probably the first thing you do when you're learning to program and it carries on.

56
00:05:05,590 --> 00:05:14,150
And then there's the grown-up version of putting more print statements in which is structured logging and open telemetry and things like that.

57
00:05:14,150 --> 00:05:18,790
I'd say that is common to all languages and all paradigms of programming.

58
00:05:18,790 --> 00:05:26,870
When you get into different more advanced tooling, I think there's often analoges, but it's different.

59
00:05:26,870 --> 00:05:37,189
So most languages have a decent debugger and the tool we call a debugger is only one aspect of debugging, but it typically has some core operations. It lets you step through code.

60
00:05:37,189 --> 00:05:39,590
It lets you print variables.

61
00:05:39,590 --> 00:05:43,670
The way that works is very different depending on your language and your runtime.

62
00:05:43,670 --> 00:05:52,390
So interpreted languages tend to need to tackle these problems very differently to compiled languages and different languages have different takes on these as well.

63
00:05:52,390 --> 00:05:59,110
Same goes I think for any kind of mechanical tracing any performance monitoring.

64
00:05:59,110 --> 00:06:04,550
Most of them also have tools that let you time travel debug these days.

65
00:06:04,550 --> 00:06:10,469
But again, the exact implementation and the technique can vary depending on what language you're talking about.

66
00:06:10,469 --> 00:06:15,590
One last point actually is the distributed case where you've got multiple processes.

67
00:06:15,590 --> 00:06:17,990
I would say that is just hard.

68
00:06:17,990 --> 00:06:21,670
One thing about a monolith, it's a lot of code to understand, but at least it's all in one place.

69
00:06:21,670 --> 00:06:31,110
Once you start having multiple interacting systems, that is another level of complexity to kind of wrangle and manage, even though the individual pieces might be simpler.

70
00:06:31,110 --> 00:06:40,150
So I think now let's move on to the part about using AI for debugging and it's been now about 2 years

71
00:06:40,150 --> 00:06:50,150
where we've been using a lot of LLMs to generate code and what do you do once the code is produced you need to debug.

72
00:06:50,150 --> 00:06:54,071
So in your opinion how can AI be used to debug? Is it promising?

73
00:06:54,071 --> 00:06:58,469
What's the I think message from the field and the work that you've been doing.

74
00:06:58,479 --> 00:07:05,670
My sense is that using LLMs for debugging is quite early.

75
00:07:05,670 --> 00:07:09,029
The thing is you mention 2 years.

76
00:07:09,029 --> 00:07:18,070
In some ways it feels like they've been with us forever at this point and in other ways every week there's a new announcement or a new change.

77
00:07:18,070 --> 00:07:23,110
So it is literally quite early in the field of debugging that goes back to I guess the first computers.

78
00:07:23,110 --> 00:07:29,909
That said, there are lots of places where it looks like LLMs should be good.

79
00:07:29,909 --> 00:07:36,629
So by LLM, I mean large language model based AI, the kind of predominant implementation at the moment.

80
00:07:36,629 --> 00:07:44,870
I would say everything that they can help with in debugging boils down to one of two things.

81
00:07:44,870 --> 00:07:50,390
I reserved my right to change my mind on this, but right now I'd say one, sifting.

82
00:07:50,390 --> 00:08:00,629
So you've got large quantities of maybe partially structured data riffling through it and finding the right bits, the nuggets that you actually need to know about or identifying patterns.

83
00:08:00,629 --> 00:08:04,070
And the other thing is automation.

84
00:08:04,070 --> 00:08:13,430
So the ability to accomplish a set of tasks that would otherwise require toil from you and distract you from the productive work of actually understanding what's going on.

85
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:19,350
Maybe there's a world where the LLM fully solves the bug for you.

86
00:08:19,350 --> 00:08:30,150
But actually, I think an important thing to remember with all of this is that it's great if they can sometimes do a tedious task end to end for you, but they're tools and their assistants.

87
00:08:30,150 --> 00:08:33,350
So, what we really need to say is how can they help?

88
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:38,790
So, let's explore the part of the AI being a debugging assistant.

89
00:08:38,790 --> 00:08:46,310
And here I wanted to ask you in your opinion is it more useful for beginners in a programming language

90
00:08:46,310 --> 00:08:56,230
who need guidance to use this assistance or is it even good for experienced developers or senior engineers to accelerate complex investigations?

91
00:08:56,240 --> 00:09:03,910
I would almost always answer both for can a tool help beginners and experts.

92
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:07,430
They potentially help in different ways.

93
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:17,910
What I'd say is that beginners maybe at programming or you can be a beginner anytime you start a new job or move to a new team.

94
00:09:17,910 --> 00:09:32,230
Beginners can benefit from tools that help them manage the complexity of everything they're seeing and understand nuances of the code base or little details that they hadn't appreciated or haven't had time to absorb yet.

95
00:09:32,230 --> 00:09:35,350
So I think there's a lot of potential there for legitimate help.

96
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:47,190
There is also a shallower help which is also important to everyone at some point which is just answer how to do this or please run this tool for me I can't be bothered

97
00:09:47,190 --> 00:09:57,590
to figure out how to write the bash script that's also valid I think for people at any level of expertise if you're an expert I think say an expert in programming generally

98
00:09:57,590 --> 00:10:07,671
or your chosen domain or maybe an expert in a codebase I think there's still help for you some of it will be the same you're a beginner every time you go to a new part of the codebase. Some of it will be different.

99
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:14,870
So potentially you'd be using it for more sophisticated questions or more sophisticated automations.

100
00:10:14,870 --> 00:10:26,630
The other dimension in this is are you an expert at prompting because all of these LLMs thrive on correct context and high quality context.

101
00:10:26,630 --> 00:10:33,590
And a big part of that is ask them the right question with the right details included so they can give you a good answer.

102
00:10:33,590 --> 00:10:39,110
So there's this extra dimension of if you can be good at that then you can be better at everything else.

103
00:10:39,120 --> 00:10:41,590
Great.

104
00:10:41,590 --> 00:10:48,710
I really like the line where you said you could be a beginner when you're looking at a new piece of code through even for an experienced person.

105
00:10:48,710 --> 00:10:55,350
Yeah, I'll go on to this next question which was based on what the answer you just gave.

106
00:10:55,350 --> 00:10:59,829
If you look at wbe coding right now where it's generating large chunks of code,

107
00:10:59,829 --> 00:11:07,829
I've been using it a lot recently for producing some user interface code which is not my area of expertise.

108
00:11:07,829 --> 00:11:13,670
And one of the areas which I found very useful was since this generates a lot of code and then I run into some problems.

109
00:11:13,670 --> 00:11:24,711
Sometimes I copy paste the messages of the errors onto my VS code screen and then I ask the LLM to tell me what might be the cause of this error. And I've seen it's pretty good.

110
00:11:24,720 --> 00:11:31,030
Right now I don't really need to go to Google or Stack Overflow to find this information.

111
00:11:31,030 --> 00:11:33,030
I'm using my coding assistant to help me with that.

112
00:11:33,030 --> 00:11:35,190
In a similar way,

113
00:11:35,190 --> 00:11:46,069
I guess for debugging, would it also make sense that you can copy paste an error or some other things from their call log and it can help you find out trace out what the problem is?

114
00:11:46,079 --> 00:11:48,870
Yes, I think so.

115
00:11:48,870 --> 00:11:57,910
Where I first found that LLMs were particularly useful in my development flow is effectively a better search for certain kind of things.

116
00:11:57,910 --> 00:12:03,269
A much superior search to what I could do for Google and the kind of problems that

117
00:12:03,279 --> 00:12:14,710
I would find it applied best to is where I want to search not just on keywords but on the meaning of the keywords and the context of the keywords.

118
00:12:14,710 --> 00:12:25,990
So my previous approach to that had to be hope that somebody has put all the keywords together with the context that's relevant in Stack Overflow and then my Google search finds it often.

119
00:12:25,990 --> 00:12:31,509
Now I can ask an LLM and I can include a lot of semantic information.

120
00:12:31,509 --> 00:12:35,750
So I can say this is what I'm trying to achieve or this is what I believe the code is doing.

121
00:12:35,750 --> 00:12:37,990
This is the message that I'm dealing with.

122
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:42,389
Please give me the relevant information for that case.

123
00:12:42,389 --> 00:12:45,030
And because at least in my very crude understanding of LLMs,

124
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:56,949
they are translating all the tokens I gave into some sort of highdimensional meaning space, they can find the thing which means what I meant very very effectively.

125
00:12:56,949 --> 00:13:01,750
So yes, I think they are potentially fantastic for that sort of thing.

126
00:13:01,750 --> 00:13:11,910
Once you bring in coding agents and the ability to act on your system and act on your codebase as well, they have the ability to search your codebase for relevant information and populate that context window with other stuff.

127
00:13:11,920 --> 00:13:15,910
And then that's maybe another dimension to debugging more effectively.

128
00:13:15,920 --> 00:13:17,829
Okay.

129
00:13:17,829 --> 00:13:21,030
So the searching is one angle where your debugging assistant can help.

130
00:13:21,040 --> 00:13:31,590
The other angle which I wanted to ask you was LLMs and coding agents are now being used to generate a lot of test cases.

131
00:13:31,590 --> 00:13:34,550
Could this also be used for debugging assistance?

132
00:13:34,550 --> 00:13:42,150
And here I have an example where suppose I have a null pointer exception in a service running in production.

133
00:13:42,150 --> 00:13:45,750
Would an LLM assisted test case help me narrow down the cause?

134
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:48,949
I think so.

135
00:13:48,949 --> 00:13:55,430
The challenge with test cases is often getting them written at all.

136
00:13:55,430 --> 00:13:59,189
A lot of developers nowadays, I think, appreciate test-driven development.

137
00:13:59,189 --> 00:14:07,829
And so I think the situation for tests is a lot better than it was, but still it is a truism that things are underested.

138
00:14:07,829 --> 00:14:09,509
Tests are not written when they should be.

139
00:14:09,509 --> 00:14:11,670
The discipline is important.

140
00:14:11,670 --> 00:14:20,230
So I think the first thing that LLMs might do is help us populate our tests sooner so that these problems don't get out there.

141
00:14:20,230 --> 00:14:30,629
But certainly once you've got something in production, you've got an issue you need to replicate, feels intuitively reasonable that LLMs could get involved all the way along.

142
00:14:30,629 --> 00:14:32,710
So I would think this is actually more of a continuum.

143
00:14:32,720 --> 00:14:46,389
probably you might use the LLM to help write a test case in the first place and try and try and provoke a bug or actually also usefully write tests for things you suspect are the problem and rule them out.

144
00:14:46,389 --> 00:14:51,990
But even then, once you've maybe managed to replicate something, you've still got to understand it.

145
00:14:51,990 --> 00:14:55,189
And at that point, what you need is your full suite of tooling.

146
00:14:55,199 --> 00:15:00,069
So there's log analysis again, there's performance analysis, there's debuggers.

147
00:15:00,079 --> 00:15:09,590
Another thing then that LLMs could do for you there is help bring together all of those tools.

148
00:15:09,590 --> 00:15:19,509
And by the way, I'd say testing is another part of the large definition of debugging I gave earlier and writing tests because it helps you understand what and why of the code.

149
00:15:19,509 --> 00:15:21,509
I think there's two sides.

150
00:15:21,509 --> 00:15:30,470
So AI can make things a lot worse for us in the sense that you don't need 30 years of development and a team of a thousand people to make a legacy codebase.

151
00:15:30,470 --> 00:15:41,350
You can vibe it now, but I can also make it a lot better by taking away toil, but also by giving us a smoother transition between tools.

152
00:15:41,350 --> 00:15:49,590
So LLMs are actually very eager to use tools these days, and they don't have the psychological barriers to learning them that humans do.

153
00:15:49,590 --> 00:15:53,749
So maybe in this case you could say well LLM I have a problem here.

154
00:15:53,749 --> 00:15:56,231
Please write a test case to replicate it. It's done that.

155
00:15:56,231 --> 00:16:05,670
It deploys maybe some more logging into production for you and you isolate more closely by analyzing the logs that come out.

156
00:16:05,670 --> 00:16:10,150
Then perhaps you have to investigate that in more detail outside production.

157
00:16:10,150 --> 00:16:14,550
Maybe using a debugger more detailed logging again you allow the LLM to iterate on it.

158
00:16:14,550 --> 00:16:23,430
So it can potentially help you all the way through this flow and take away a load of things that individually would have been distractions to the task of understanding.

159
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:25,031
I like that. That's great.

160
00:16:25,031 --> 00:16:34,470
So you can have almost have an LLM as your interface between the different tools and help you find stuff and then pipe it back

161
00:16:34,470 --> 00:16:40,710
to another tool and help with the understanding of the debugging problem that you're trying to solve.

162
00:16:40,710 --> 00:16:44,150
So, let's talk about debugging strategies.

163
00:16:44,150 --> 00:16:48,870
Is that something that your AI debugging assistant can help with?

164
00:16:48,880 --> 00:16:52,389
I think yes.

165
00:16:52,389 --> 00:16:58,310
And actually, one very simple way we found they can help is they're a better rubber duck.

166
00:16:58,310 --> 00:17:04,150
In our office, developers are actually we've got some rubber ducks that used to lie around in the office.

167
00:17:04,150 --> 00:17:10,949
developers have actually used those and tried to explain an issue to them and in the process solved it.

168
00:17:10,949 --> 00:17:14,549
Imagine if the rubber duck had a lot of software engineering expertise as well.

169
00:17:14,549 --> 00:17:16,710
You could just bounce ideas back and forth.

170
00:17:16,710 --> 00:17:22,470
So I think that's the first step actually is just giving you ideas that you wouldn't have thought of on your own.

171
00:17:22,470 --> 00:17:25,669
Doesn't have to solve the thing for you.

172
00:17:25,669 --> 00:17:39,510
Then identifying different possible tools and different ways of applying things is another one in the maybe slightly longer term as people are using LLM based agents more as well.

173
00:17:39,510 --> 00:17:50,950
We touched on how the AI could be your core interface to things and I think one of the challenges in a debugging strategy is staying in your flow state.

174
00:17:50,950 --> 00:17:56,710
So there's a reason that people love logging and it's because it's programming and they're already doing that.

175
00:17:56,710 --> 00:18:00,230
So you've just written some code, you want to know what it did, you can type in a few more lines.

176
00:18:00,230 --> 00:18:04,150
That might take if you've got a big C++ codebase, it might take a few hours to rebuild.

177
00:18:04,160 --> 00:18:08,070
You go and have a sword fight on a desk chair, but you're still programming.

178
00:18:08,080 --> 00:18:09,990
You're still in your flow state.

179
00:18:09,990 --> 00:18:23,029
I think a potentially very valuable thing AI agents could do is once your flow state is a conversation with the agent, transitioning much more seamlessly into other debugging strategies.

180
00:18:23,029 --> 00:18:37,510
So instead of you having to get your head out of coding space and think about Perf or think about GDB or think about whatever your logging framework is even if it's complicated, just say okay, what should I do next?

181
00:18:37,510 --> 00:18:39,510
Please look at the performance logs.

182
00:18:39,510 --> 00:18:44,311
please gather a time travel recording and correlate them for me. And you stay in your flow.

183
00:18:44,311 --> 00:18:55,510
You stay in your vibing mindset rather than having to transition between all of these different command syntaxes and output formats, etc. That's interesting.

184
00:18:55,510 --> 00:19:00,230
So, a way to maintain your context or you don't have to do so much context switching, right?

185
00:19:00,240 --> 00:19:01,751
Exactly. Yeah.

186
00:19:01,760 --> 00:19:03,830
That's great.

187
00:19:03,830 --> 00:19:06,549
Since I've got you on the show, I had to ask this question.

188
00:19:06,549 --> 00:19:12,150
Kernel level bugs are supposed to be very difficult to fix.

189
00:19:12,150 --> 00:19:13,830
Can a debug assistant help with this?

190
00:19:13,840 --> 00:19:17,510
I think yes.

191
00:19:17,510 --> 00:19:27,029
In my programming and kernel level stuff, it's mostly been on the Linux kernel or on Linux derived kernel level code.

192
00:19:27,029 --> 00:19:30,549
And I've not yet tried applying an LLM to that.

193
00:19:30,549 --> 00:19:40,870
But my expectation would be that it will actually be a very good experience because the LLM is potentially in its training data set encountered parts of Linux code.

194
00:19:40,870 --> 00:19:53,510
It certainly will have encountered documentation about it, mailing list, discussions, etc. So it will know the context about kernel code that I do not or that isn't in my head right now.

195
00:19:53,510 --> 00:19:57,990
And then it's also very good at understanding a big complex code base which of course kernels typically are.

196
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:07,350
So I can see it being very helpful from that side, maybe even for generating some of the code if you can get it to understand the right rules.

197
00:20:07,350 --> 00:20:16,470
There's a lot of written and unwritten rules in kernel programming, but if you can get those in place, I think it could be very useful there.

198
00:20:16,470 --> 00:20:22,950
The thing that I'm not aware of anyone having tried is trying to automate your debugging flow.

199
00:20:22,950 --> 00:20:32,789
So possibly within easy reach would be add some logging statements, rebuild and reboot some remote machine and then see what comes out.

200
00:20:32,789 --> 00:20:35,270
I think you could do that.

201
00:20:35,270 --> 00:20:43,190
The really spicy thing I think would be hooking up an LLM to a kernel mode debugger and having it step through the kernel code on another machine.

202
00:20:43,190 --> 00:20:47,110
I really haven't heard of anyone doing that.

203
00:20:47,110 --> 00:20:50,870
I'd love to find out if anyone has because that sounds well it sounds awesome.

204
00:20:50,870 --> 00:20:54,549
It also sounds like an absolute nightmare to manage. So I'd be very interested to see what could do there.

205
00:20:54,559 --> 00:20:57,990
But eventually I imagine that's what it will be like.

206
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:02,149
So now that we've looked at that, I wanted to ask you another question.

207
00:21:02,149 --> 00:21:15,270
When we always talk about LLM use cases that we've seen on literature and also on blog posts even for the debugging aspects the languages are predominantly in like Python, JavaScript, Go, Java.

208
00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:22,549
I've never seen that much about C and C++.

209
00:21:22,549 --> 00:21:30,549
What is your experience with using AI assistance for coding as well as say debugging C and C++ code?

210
00:21:30,549 --> 00:21:38,710
These days a lot of my coding is actually in Python and in sort of the glue levels above these low-level systems.

211
00:21:38,710 --> 00:21:47,270
So I've been using coding assistants in various forms to help me with that and I found it it's very useful.

212
00:21:47,270 --> 00:22:00,630
One of the advantages I believe that LLMs have for languages that are perhaps more modern and perhaps more scripting oriented is that there's a lot of code out in the public they can be trained on.

213
00:22:00,630 --> 00:22:02,710
So they are excellent at understanding those languages.

214
00:22:02,710 --> 00:22:15,510
The flip side is that I've also heard I haven't experienced this myself but they can get a bit muddled with what is valid code in a dynamic language.

215
00:22:15,510 --> 00:22:30,470
So languages like JavaScript and Python, you don't have the guard rails of the compiler telling you, "No, don't do that. That's wrong." When you do something bad with the type system, and that's potentially a weakness.

216
00:22:30,470 --> 00:22:40,630
So the nice thing, I guess, for compiled languages like C and C++ is that you do have the compiler there to give the LLM a telling off and say, "No, you can't do that.

217
00:22:40,630 --> 00:22:47,590
That doesn't type check. Try it again." And it gives the LLM some guard rails, which is always good.

218
00:22:47,590 --> 00:22:56,950
I think one of the things they need is to be grounded in some sort of truth about the system so that they can keep being pulled back to that rather than hallucinating.

219
00:22:56,950 --> 00:23:05,990
And the other thing they need is good quality context about what they're doing and what's going on right now.

220
00:23:05,990 --> 00:23:17,190
So in terms of the context actually my experience is that coding agents already pretty good at finding that context in I suspect any languages code base. They know how to navigate different programming languages.

221
00:23:17,190 --> 00:23:21,029
They know how to navigate a project structure.

222
00:23:21,029 --> 00:23:27,990
And there's enough C and C++ out there that they are decently good at generating it and understanding it as well.

223
00:23:27,990 --> 00:23:35,590
I guess it's possible that there are some shortcomings I haven't seen yet, but certainly they seem effective from everything I've tried.

224
00:23:35,590 --> 00:23:44,310
The only thing I'd say is that C and C++ tend to also be associated with big scary legacy code bases

225
00:23:44,310 --> 00:23:53,830
and they tend to have very unfortunate patterns of bugs and they tend not to have standard logging frameworks and so it does create a load of challenges you might not see in other languages.

226
00:23:53,840 --> 00:23:58,070
Mhm.

227
00:23:58,070 --> 00:24:00,549
like heap uh errors and memory buffer overflows and all that good stuff.

228
00:24:00,549 --> 00:24:03,910
Yeah, exactly. Yes.

229
00:24:03,910 --> 00:24:08,630
So, so few rules in C and C++ compared to what you can rely on in other programming languages.

230
00:24:08,630 --> 00:24:15,190
It's part of what makes it fun and it's part of what makes it effective for kernel level programming, but it is a double-edged sword.

231
00:24:15,200 --> 00:24:17,029
Okay.

232
00:24:17,029 --> 00:24:20,149
So, let's now go into some of the tooling for debugging.

233
00:24:20,149 --> 00:24:26,710
And one of the things that actually you pointed me to when I was researching for this show was this tool called chat dbg.

234
00:24:26,710 --> 00:24:29,590
I don't know if is it chat debugger or chat dbg.

235
00:24:29,590 --> 00:24:32,630
What is it? Maybe could you explain that to our listeners?

236
00:24:32,640 --> 00:24:37,830
Sure.

237
00:24:37,830 --> 00:24:39,669
So chat dbg is a research paper originally.

238
00:24:39,669 --> 00:24:46,549
The title of the research paper is augmenting debugging with large language models.

239
00:24:46,549 --> 00:24:52,630
and that's out of the University of Massachusetts, Amhurst, AWS, and Williams College.

240
00:24:52,630 --> 00:24:57,669
What they did was hook up various software debuggers,

241
00:24:57,679 --> 00:25:08,390
the conventional forward stepping variable printing debuggers we are all used to and have all used at some point to LLM.

242
00:25:08,390 --> 00:25:16,950
And I think back when they actually published this originally, they were using the new tool calling abilities of LLM.

243
00:25:16,950 --> 00:25:28,310
So one of the things that's become, I think, quite revolutionary in AI in the last year or two is the ability for the AI to call external tools.

244
00:25:28,310 --> 00:25:37,590
And that gives the AI the ability to populate its own context window with relevant things and to access the ground truth about the outside world.

245
00:25:37,590 --> 00:25:41,190
So what they've done is they said,

246
00:25:41,190 --> 00:25:53,510
well, what if the LLM had access to a software debugger and now it can monitor the behavior of the code using that software debugger and gain deeper insights into it.

247
00:25:53,510 --> 00:26:05,110
And moreover, what if we then say, well, the user can ask questions not about how to run the debugger, but about the actual behavior of the program itself.

248
00:26:05,110 --> 00:26:09,830
So eventually you can just ask one of their examples, why is X null here?

249
00:26:09,830 --> 00:26:15,430
So it's natural language, which is nice, but it's also a higher level kind of question.

250
00:26:15,430 --> 00:26:21,510
You're not having to compose the operations required in the debugger to answer the question.

251
00:26:21,510 --> 00:26:29,029
You just say the thing you want to know and it's almost more like a query than operating an interactive tool. Now, well, that's great.

252
00:26:29,029 --> 00:26:40,150
So, it's like when you're debugging in your call stack, you can have like a natural language where you can pose a question through the LLM and then it'll find it out and reply back to you. Right.

253
00:26:40,159 --> 00:26:43,909
Exactly. Yes. Okay, that's cool.

254
00:26:43,909 --> 00:26:45,909
I think something that we would all be looking forward to.

255
00:26:45,919 --> 00:26:57,590
And taking off from there, one of the questions I had based on the previous answers where you said it's possible for the AI

256
00:26:57,590 --> 00:27:01,990
to actually go between languages and almost also between different tooling, right?

257
00:27:01,990 --> 00:27:09,510
If you had a very large system that's built on different components like what we typically have nowadays, we have some scripting in Python,

258
00:27:09,510 --> 00:27:14,789
we have a back end in Java, we have a front end in Vue.js or React or whatever.

259
00:27:14,789 --> 00:27:16,950
And usually a bug kind of spans between all these boundaries.

260
00:27:16,950 --> 00:27:29,909
Do you think something like this chat DBG could help us track bugs across multiple languages and then show us a recommended approach to fix the problem in an affected module?

261
00:27:29,919 --> 00:27:32,230
I think that would be very interesting.

262
00:27:32,240 --> 00:27:40,630
I'm not aware of currently any AI agent that will combine all of the complicated parts of that.

263
00:27:40,630 --> 00:27:46,789
So the multiple languages, the distributed nature of it, complex interactions.

264
00:27:46,789 --> 00:27:51,110
Chat DBG, I think it has several different debugger backends.

265
00:27:51,110 --> 00:27:55,750
So you could maybe imagine it talking to a C component and to a Python component and to some other components.

266
00:27:55,760 --> 00:28:03,990
The challenge for debugging a distributed system though is also that you need to allow it to run.

267
00:28:03,990 --> 00:28:12,549
So using live debuggers that step can be difficult in a distributed system even when you've solved the problems of can I cover all the languages

268
00:28:12,549 --> 00:28:22,230
that I need can I understand the interactions because if you stop one of them then timeouts can happen or you can change you can radically change the order in which things happen.

269
00:28:22,230 --> 00:28:26,310
So it's a it's a challenging area.

270
00:28:26,310 --> 00:28:34,470
I also suspect that for quite a while doing this well this sort of very varied problem is still going to need human guidance as well

271
00:28:34,480 --> 00:28:46,389
because there's a lot of different things you need the LLM to be smart for and my general experience has been it's best to give it one thing to be smart about at once.

272
00:28:46,389 --> 00:28:52,710
Trying to get it to balance lots of different tasks from lots of different sources without some guard rails is challenging.

273
00:28:52,710 --> 00:29:02,149
So you either need guardrails to come from your system or you need them to come from the human and I think it's it's going to be a case of both of those for some time to come.

274
00:29:02,159 --> 00:29:03,669
Yeah.

275
00:29:03,669 --> 00:29:09,029
So I think that is a bit of a complicated use case but it's maybe something that will be solved in the future.

276
00:29:09,029 --> 00:29:14,071
I'll move on to the next question which is I wanted to know about time travel debugging. What is it?

277
00:29:14,071 --> 00:29:17,430
time travel debugging.

278
00:29:17,430 --> 00:29:20,310
It's a vision for how you should debug software first of all.

279
00:29:20,320 --> 00:29:28,470
And the vision is that you shouldn't have to pick and choose what information you get like you do with logging.

280
00:29:28,470 --> 00:29:30,870
You should have it all by default.

281
00:29:30,870 --> 00:29:39,430
So what time travel debuggers have in common is the ability to record everything your program did and then replay it deterministically.

282
00:29:39,440 --> 00:29:43,750
Typically they can do that in reverse as well.

283
00:29:43,750 --> 00:29:45,669
So you can actually rewind which I'll come back to.

284
00:29:45,669 --> 00:29:47,750
The trick with time travel debugging is making it efficient.

285
00:29:47,760 --> 00:29:52,630
Modern time travel debugging systems are actually very efficient.

286
00:29:52,630 --> 00:29:57,910
So they don't need to single step the program and record every instruction that ran anymore. That would be very bad.

287
00:29:57,910 --> 00:30:01,510
That would be, you know, higher overhead than detailed logging.

288
00:30:01,510 --> 00:30:11,590
What they do instead is they use a variety of lower level tricks in the system to capture only what affects the nondeterministic behaviors at execution time

289
00:30:11,590 --> 00:30:16,630
and then replay just those and you can recomputee every intermediate state.

290
00:30:16,630 --> 00:30:29,669
So it means every memory location at every machine instruction that ran is available to you now and what you need to do is then select the variables you want and that's where the reverse execution comes in.

291
00:30:29,669 --> 00:30:35,430
So I like to say that normal debuggers tell you what because they're like a microscope.

292
00:30:35,430 --> 00:30:40,070
They let you inspect all of the state in your program and understand exactly what is going on right now.

293
00:30:40,070 --> 00:30:46,310
A time travel debugger gives you access to causality I guess.

294
00:30:46,310 --> 00:30:51,350
So you can say how did we get here and that means taking you from what to why.

295
00:30:51,350 --> 00:30:59,350
So the real big benefit is to be able to query backwards in time and say well how did this value get set in the past?

296
00:30:59,350 --> 00:31:05,190
How did we get into this function call and why did we get in now?

297
00:31:05,190 --> 00:31:10,070
So, it's a very broad set of data almost in a way the broadest set of data you could have about your program and

298
00:31:10,080 --> 00:31:22,470
you query it to answer questions all the way from conventional debugging problems to performance problems to stuff that you might otherwise have used logging for but that needs a rebuild.

299
00:31:22,480 --> 00:31:24,230
Okay, great.

300
00:31:24,230 --> 00:31:30,389
So does it work with the trace data that that we usually use like our logs and traces? Does it work with that?

301
00:31:30,399 --> 00:31:38,549
Time travel debug systems usually they work at a lower level than that typically.

302
00:31:38,549 --> 00:31:43,590
So there are some time travel like systems which use something like trace data to reconstruct states.

303
00:31:43,590 --> 00:31:48,950
But the trouble is in those systems you can only reconstruct what was traced.

304
00:31:48,950 --> 00:31:51,909
That's often not everything.

305
00:31:51,909 --> 00:32:06,630
So time travel debugging systems tend to be implemented at a lower level either at the level of the programming languages runtime particularly for interpreted languages or as some sort of just in time recompilation for native languages.

306
00:32:06,630 --> 00:32:16,389
So they tend to sit under the level of your code and that's what gives them the power to inspect and capture everything it does efficiently.

307
00:32:16,389 --> 00:32:18,310
What you can do is combine techniques.

308
00:32:18,310 --> 00:32:27,269
So potentially you could take a time travel debuggers recording and you could extract the same information you usually would have got from tracing.

309
00:32:27,269 --> 00:32:42,149
Is there a lot of plumbing that needs to be done to support this or typically no actually the integrations with time travel debuggers are very simple and I'd say it's for similar reasons to the phenomenon where you say

310
00:32:42,159 --> 00:32:49,990
well I want to run my code in a virtual machine now or I want to run my code in the container now and you just lift it up and put it there and it works.

311
00:32:49,990 --> 00:32:58,470
The fact that the integration of a time travel debugging system is below the level of your code means you don't explicitly need to change anything.

312
00:32:58,470 --> 00:33:03,269
You just feed an extra layer into the system and you get that extra visibility.

313
00:33:03,279 --> 00:33:05,351
Okay. Interesting.

314
00:33:05,351 --> 00:33:13,430
So it's like another question cuz I'm a bit fascinated with this is the fact that it keeps track of say is it at the register level like what gets written

315
00:33:13,430 --> 00:33:21,190
to the register something like that or a bit more higher for time travel debugging systems that work at machine instruction level.

316
00:33:21,190 --> 00:33:26,950
Yes, it's it's register level state and memory level.

317
00:33:26,950 --> 00:33:28,630
But the important thing is tracking that would be horrible.

318
00:33:28,640 --> 00:33:35,029
Tracking your register state for every machine instruction would be a nightmare.

319
00:33:35,029 --> 00:33:44,950
So what they actually do in practice and this is true across a variety of systems is they capture what was the starting state of your program at a low level.

320
00:33:44,950 --> 00:33:52,950
So the registers and memory, what information got into your program from the outside world and then everything else can be recomputed.

321
00:33:52,950 --> 00:34:01,830
And there's a load of clever tricks you do to make the recomputation efficient because you don't want to replay everything you recorded every time you want to ask a question.

322
00:34:01,830 --> 00:34:04,950
But fundamentally,

323
00:34:04,950 --> 00:34:15,589
you actually only need to know what influenced the runtime because modern CPUs are immensely good at rerunning deterministic code very very quickly.

324
00:34:15,589 --> 00:34:19,430
You don't need to be capturing all of that stuff and actually it's lower overhead not to.

325
00:34:19,430 --> 00:34:21,190
So, it's actually it's smoke and mirrors.

326
00:34:21,190 --> 00:34:27,430
We call it time travel debugging, but the real technique underneath the hood is deterministic record and replay.

327
00:34:27,430 --> 00:34:37,669
And then everything else is kind of magic tricks to provide a better user interface so that it looks like a debugger or it looks like a logging system or it looks like a tool an AI agent can use.

328
00:34:37,679 --> 00:34:45,430
That's great because I would like to just end this section of by asking you a question which I saw on the Undo website

329
00:34:45,440 --> 00:34:55,750
which is that would you be able to point me to what caused a crash 15 years back and the developer who wrote the code has left the company?

330
00:34:55,750 --> 00:34:59,430
Could time travel debugging help with this kind of a problem?

331
00:34:59,440 --> 00:35:02,310
Absolutely.

332
00:35:02,310 --> 00:35:08,230
So, I think the reason that that's a good example is because it's legacy code.

333
00:35:08,230 --> 00:35:15,990
It's a huge system and it's something that just starts to happen particularly in big organizations when they've been developing for a while.

334
00:35:15,990 --> 00:35:24,870
And it happens even in the most or perhaps especially the most missionritical important code bases people have

335
00:35:24,870 --> 00:35:33,990
because over time you have thousands of people work on these they work in different generations of programming languages different paradigms

336
00:35:33,990 --> 00:35:46,310
and there's lots of domain specific expertise and as we said earlier anytime you go into code that you didn't write you're a beginner again particularly if it's a large body of work.

337
00:35:46,310 --> 00:35:52,069
So the reason that time travel debugging helps in these cases is it allows you to see the causality.

338
00:35:52,069 --> 00:36:00,630
So you don't have to understand your 10 million line code base in detail to infer how a bug happened.

339
00:36:00,630 --> 00:36:03,109
Instead, you can rewind through it.

340
00:36:03,109 --> 00:36:06,711
So you could say, well, this value was bad. Why was it bad?

341
00:36:06,711 --> 00:36:08,791
Rewind to where it last changed. Oh, okay.

342
00:36:08,791 --> 00:36:10,470
I didn't expect to be in that code path.

343
00:36:10,480 --> 00:36:12,470
Why are we there?

344
00:36:12,470 --> 00:36:16,870
And so you can rewind again and find why the decisions were taken there.

345
00:36:16,870 --> 00:36:29,589
And what it means is that a lot of the domain specific knowledge that you might have needed to ask your colleague who left 15 years ago actually can be recaptured by understanding what really happened.

346
00:36:29,589 --> 00:36:31,670
And stuff you didn't need to know.

347
00:36:31,670 --> 00:36:37,430
Like theories you had about the bug that were wrong, you don't need to worry about anymore because you can see that those things didn't happen.

348
00:36:37,440 --> 00:36:50,310
The interesting thing here, and it took us a while to realize this even at Undo, is that the problem we're managing for developers here is very similar to the problems you're managing for an AI.

349
00:36:50,310 --> 00:36:54,310
So, it's provide them with a ground truth of what really happened in the system.

350
00:36:54,320 --> 00:36:56,550
Provide them tools to navigate it.

351
00:36:56,560 --> 00:37:01,990
Provide them with the right context and highquality relevant context.

352
00:37:01,990 --> 00:37:06,390
And don't give them irrelevant information because it will confuse them.

353
00:37:06,390 --> 00:37:12,870
is actually very very similar phenomena to what we all have when we're trying to get good output from AIs.

354
00:37:12,870 --> 00:37:19,430
It's just that humans are much better intelligences and so you know the levels of context they can cope with are smaller.

355
00:37:19,430 --> 00:37:27,190
They can have you know less relevant information they can fix it for themselves and then they can ultimately have a lot more in their head at once.

356
00:37:27,190 --> 00:37:37,829
I think it's pretty fascinating and I think maybe we will have to add some more show notes on time travel debugging and examples from some of the blogs that I read on on.

357
00:37:37,829 --> 00:37:47,349
So let me go on to the last portion of the show where I want to talk a little bit about autonomous agents for debugging and what exactly we mean here.

358
00:37:47,349 --> 00:37:52,630
I want your take because when I think about autonomous agents for debugging.

359
00:37:52,630 --> 00:38:00,230
It appears to me like there's an agent which does the debugging which automatically creates the break points

360
00:38:00,230 --> 00:38:08,710
which steps through the code finds the issue and you know somehow magically displays that on the screen to me.

361
00:38:08,710 --> 00:38:11,190
What is your take on an autonomous agent for debugging?

362
00:38:11,190 --> 00:38:16,710
So, first of all, I'd like to define what I would say an AI agent is.

363
00:38:16,710 --> 00:38:23,349
And it's something that can act on its own independently of you.

364
00:38:23,349 --> 00:38:31,670
So, it can decide to tackle certain tasks or run certain tools and then adapt to their responses in pursuit of a wider goal.

365
00:38:31,670 --> 00:38:37,270
And it's doing that autonomously, but on your behalf.

366
00:38:37,270 --> 00:38:39,510
So, it is acting for you in some ways, your agent.

367
00:38:39,510 --> 00:38:46,230
The most common kind of agent we as developers see is the coding agent.

368
00:38:46,230 --> 00:38:58,310
These have sort of evolved from what we called coding assistance where it was an incredibly powerful but glorified autocomplete into something where it can accomplish software engineering tasks on its own.

369
00:38:58,310 --> 00:39:05,030
That's broadly I think for debugging where things are starting.

370
00:39:05,030 --> 00:39:12,069
Coding agents as we've said debugging is a lot of what coding actually is and coding agents have taken that on board as well.

371
00:39:12,069 --> 00:39:17,589
They can do debugging but it's fairly early days.

372
00:39:17,589 --> 00:39:23,750
The interesting thing I see is using a coding agent Claude code for instance.

373
00:39:23,750 --> 00:39:32,870
I've tried to debug a sample problem in the past and spoilers I was trying to get it to use a debugger because I thought time travel debugging would help.

374
00:39:32,870 --> 00:39:40,870
But early on what I saw it do was edit my code, add a load of print fs in places it thought was interesting and ask for permission to recompile it.

375
00:39:40,880 --> 00:39:47,829
And I mean actually if it can choose the right places to put the print fs that's potentially useful.

376
00:39:47,829 --> 00:39:54,630
Again if you have a compilation time that is seconds or minutes rather than hours it's potentially useful.

377
00:39:54,630 --> 00:40:01,750
But it did remind me of a terminator chasing a woolly mammoth and trying to bonk it on the head with a bone or something.

378
00:40:01,750 --> 00:40:09,910
is this weird juxtiposition of a very sophisticated modern tool and then pretty much the oldest debugging tool we have.

379
00:40:09,920 --> 00:40:16,550
Potentially though, I think we'll see this transition more towards more sophisticated agentic debugging,

380
00:40:16,560 --> 00:40:26,870
debugging by agents and coding agents are going to be the first place we see that thanks to in a large part actually this thing MCP,

381
00:40:26,870 --> 00:40:34,310
the model context protocol which was developed originally by Anthropic and it's taken off all over the place.

382
00:40:34,310 --> 00:40:40,870
What it amounts to, I would say, because I spent a lot of time trying to puzzle how it fits into the system.

