00:40:40,870 --> 00:40:44,630
It's a plug-in architecture kind of no more, no less.

384
00:40:44,630 --> 00:40:47,349
It plugs tools into whatever your local LLM client is.

385
00:40:47,349 --> 00:40:54,470
And there's no reason those tools can't include a debugger or a performance profiler or something else.

386
00:40:54,470 --> 00:41:03,270
The real trick with these tools, though, is how you get the AI agent to be good at using them.

387
00:41:03,270 --> 00:41:05,910
And that's partly a design challenge for people like me.

388
00:41:05,910 --> 00:41:11,670
So, how do we make our debug tooling work well with what an LLM agent needs?

389
00:41:11,670 --> 00:41:20,309
And it's partly for the AI companies as well to train better tool use into their products and more broad awareness of tools,

390
00:41:20,309 --> 00:41:25,349
better interaction with the MCP protocol and other tool use protocols.

391
00:41:25,349 --> 00:41:33,510
And what I'd expect we'll see is coding agents getting better and better and then potentially specialized agents

392
00:41:33,510 --> 00:41:40,470
for debugging certain kinds of problem as well because there's a different kind of of knowledge and flow involved in debugging.

393
00:41:40,470 --> 00:41:44,790
You mentioned you know selecting a debugging strategy earlier.

394
00:41:44,790 --> 00:41:53,991
You could imagine a a hierarchical collection of these things where maybe your coding agent spits out code and then farms out to a specialized AI. I've got this problem.

395
00:41:53,991 --> 00:41:55,750
how do we solve it?

396
00:41:55,750 --> 00:42:05,670
That tries different strategies, different tools, and aggregates the information together to feed back and then the coding agent acts on that, makes some code changes, and we try again.

397
00:42:05,680 --> 00:42:11,349
Yeah, I like that. I think we're still not there based on all of the conversations we've had so far.

398
00:42:11,349 --> 00:42:12,950
But I still had to ask you this.

399
00:42:12,950 --> 00:42:22,550
So do you think the future is something like if you have a performance issue which users are reporting but you don't really see anything in your traces

400
00:42:22,550 --> 00:42:29,670
or logs with respect to a performance issue but then it's maybe caused by a third party integration.

401
00:42:29,670 --> 00:42:37,349
Do you think the different debugging agents like you have a master debugging agent and you like you said you have lot of mini debugging agents doing different things.

402
00:42:37,349 --> 00:42:43,109
Could this be something like the master orchestrates and finds out the issue in this particular case?

403
00:42:43,109 --> 00:42:47,670
Because this typically happens where the user reports slow performance speeds,

404
00:42:47,670 --> 00:42:58,950
but we have nothing in the logs or any indication in the traces to actually show that a particular service is acting badly and then you actually find out it's not your service but it's a third party integration.

405
00:42:58,960 --> 00:43:05,990
I think so this is probably not possible yet but we'll already be seeing the glimmers of it.

406
00:43:05,990 --> 00:43:17,829
And actually I think one thing which is worth bearing in mind is that there's a perfect rosecolored spectacles world where the AIs solve all of our problems and they can solve these things end to end.

407
00:43:17,829 --> 00:43:25,829
But actually there's huge value to be had in getting them to do the boring 80% of the work to take the toil off so we can focus.

408
00:43:25,829 --> 00:43:37,750
So even if we can't solve the whole thing, actually having the LLM act as your agent again, go out and gather the information you need to make the next decision still hugely valuable.

409
00:43:37,750 --> 00:43:45,910
I think the trick to debugging issues is how you make the AI as smart as it can be.

410
00:43:45,910 --> 00:43:51,910
And the challenge for an AI debugging agent is that you have to get the right context fed in there just like a human developer,

411
00:43:51,910 --> 00:44:04,069
but more so they need to know as much as possible about what's going on because they otherwise they won't be able to answer questions or direct their investigations.

412
00:44:04,069 --> 00:44:06,790
And if they don't know stuff, they also tend to hallucinate.

413
00:44:06,800 --> 00:44:10,069
And that's something I think we've all seen at this point.

414
00:44:10,069 --> 00:44:15,829
Sometimes it's very amusing, but you don't want it happening in the middle of a production issue and sending you on a wild goose trace.

415
00:44:15,829 --> 00:44:22,550
So for this, you need the right ability to gather the information and you need that information to be solid.

416
00:44:22,550 --> 00:44:29,829
So in the kind of scenario you described, I'd imagine this is this is probably a tiered approach.

417
00:44:29,829 --> 00:44:31,910
Often debugging challenges want a range of tools.

418
00:44:31,910 --> 00:44:39,990
So you might start with your inputs are basic performance level monitoring and user input.

419
00:44:39,990 --> 00:44:41,589
So user feedback is valuable as well here.

420
00:44:41,589 --> 00:44:50,950
Once you've started investigating, I'd imagine you'd go down potentially a chain of increasingly sophisticated debug approaches.

421
00:44:50,950 --> 00:45:03,109
So you'd initially look at your tracing and you might well automate that and say, "Okay, LLM, when a performance alert goes off, look at the traces, see if there's anything weird." If there's not, then you've got a choice, I guess.

422
00:45:03,109 --> 00:45:17,510
You can go and look as a human, or you can say, "Okay, go and do the next phase." And the next phase in that world would probably be something like profiling or some lightweight capture of more detail tracing.

423
00:45:17,510 --> 00:45:23,589
But if you've got a complex problem with many moving parts or maybe you've got legacy parts of the system, maybe that's not enough as well.

424
00:45:23,589 --> 00:45:28,550
So at that point, you might move up to two potential approaches.

425
00:45:28,550 --> 00:45:32,391
One is write tests and try and replicate it out of production. That may or may not work.

426
00:45:32,391 --> 00:45:36,791
or for bugs where it's extremely complicated. You can't replicate it.

427
00:45:36,791 --> 00:45:38,630
It only happens in this place.

428
00:45:38,630 --> 00:45:51,510
That's that's where I'd say something like a time travel debugging system with its capability to fully record and capture the interactions between different services as well would be really valuable.

429
00:45:51,510 --> 00:46:04,230
So I think the LLM can help with the individual stages but ultimately the challenge we are facing at each stage is how do we make the LLM for this part of the task as smart as possible.

430
00:46:04,230 --> 00:46:13,030
So that's down to prompting giving it the right details and giving it then a ground truth about what it's reasoning about and giving it the right context.

431
00:46:13,030 --> 00:46:23,109
And the ultimate of that is when you get up to the full logs that come out of time travel debugging and it gives you the ability to verify what went through the system as well and why things happened.

432
00:46:23,109 --> 00:46:27,990
So the LLM's got the power of that but you've got the power as well to go through and check it's working.

433
00:46:28,000 --> 00:46:29,829
I think that makes a lot of sense.

434
00:46:29,829 --> 00:46:34,309
The answer that you said it has to be a layered approach.

435
00:46:34,309 --> 00:46:47,670
So let's move on to the next question I wanted to ask you is one of the worries you have with autonomous agents is you know introducing any regressions or security vulnerabilities or maybe masking the real root cause.

436
00:46:47,670 --> 00:46:55,030
The reason I asked this question is recently I remember seeing this thread on Twitter which I think orex

437
00:46:55,030 --> 00:47:03,670
which I'm sure a lot of you also saw which is with one of the databases from one of the companies where a lot of records were deleted

438
00:47:03,670 --> 00:47:11,750
and then when the autonomous agent was posed because that agent actually produced the different it inserted rows into the table and then deleted a lot of it

439
00:47:11,750 --> 00:47:21,829
and when you ask the agent probe the agent about this deletion it actually lied about it and actually came up with some fake records which also happened.

440
00:47:21,829 --> 00:47:23,829
So this is a cause of concern.

441
00:47:23,829 --> 00:47:32,069
Obviously one of the things the team did in that case I think they've added a lot of guard rails around what the agent could do and how much access it had and things like that.

442
00:47:32,079 --> 00:47:42,870
Now when you're actually sort of looking at those autonomous switch agents in the debugging context and where we're trying to solve a problem again we could run into similar issues

443
00:47:42,870 --> 00:47:54,309
right where you don't really how much do you believe the agent there's a certain level of trust but I wanted to kind of I had to pose this question to you to ask you what do you need to do to validate that what the agent is giving is actually right.

444
00:47:54,319 --> 00:47:57,750
Sure.

445
00:47:57,750 --> 00:48:09,910
It's an interesting one because yes, there's so many nightmare scenarios out there where you see somebody who said conversations like, "Why is the database empty? Why did you delete it?" And the LLM says, "You're right.

446
00:48:09,910 --> 00:48:14,950
You did tell me specifically not to delete the entire database.

447
00:48:14,950 --> 00:48:22,790
Next time, I will ensure that that doesn't happen." There's lots of opportunity for unexpected behavior still.

448
00:48:22,790 --> 00:48:29,430
Ultimately, the AI model vendors, I think, do a lot of work to try and mitigate this stuff.

449
00:48:29,440 --> 00:48:39,589
They do a lot of work with reinforcement learning to try and align the AI with don't lie to the user and don't do inadvisable things.

450
00:48:39,589 --> 00:48:42,549
Let's say follow instructions carefully.

451
00:48:42,549 --> 00:48:47,670
But the problem with them is right now it's not exactly that they're lying.

452
00:48:47,670 --> 00:48:49,589
Even they don't know they're lying.

453
00:48:49,589 --> 00:48:51,589
They know the thing they do.

454
00:48:51,589 --> 00:48:54,150
And the thing they do is try and provide you a good answer.

455
00:48:54,150 --> 00:48:56,069
And there are many parts of a good answer.

456
00:48:56,069 --> 00:49:00,549
One of them is having an authorative and polite tone.

457
00:49:00,549 --> 00:49:02,630
Another is using the correct terminology for your domain.

458
00:49:02,630 --> 00:49:06,069
Another is citing specific examples from your source code.

459
00:49:06,069 --> 00:49:08,549
And another is actually being based in truth.

460
00:49:08,549 --> 00:49:12,790
And they'll choose as many as those as they can to get you to a good answer.

461
00:49:12,790 --> 00:49:14,870
But any of them might get dropped.

462
00:49:14,870 --> 00:49:18,150
And one of the hard ones to keep is the truth.

463
00:49:18,150 --> 00:49:23,109
So that that is quite likely to be a casualty if there's not enough information.

464
00:49:23,109 --> 00:49:25,990
As we said earlier, I think guard rails are very important.

465
00:49:26,000 --> 00:49:29,349
And there's two ways you can interpret the rails as well.

466
00:49:29,349 --> 00:49:34,790
There's the there's the rails which stop you, you know, tripping over somewhere you shouldn't.

467
00:49:34,800 --> 00:49:36,870
The safety rails.

468
00:49:36,870 --> 00:49:42,230
So that would be things like controls on what operations the AI can do.

469
00:49:42,230 --> 00:49:52,549
The other is more like train tracks not in the sense of exactly controlling it but in the sense of choosing desirable paths so providing the right information to them.

470
00:49:52,549 --> 00:50:01,670
So I guess if we look at the context of introducing security vulnerabilities let's say

471
00:50:01,670 --> 00:50:08,630
you might have a guardrail which is certain kinds of security scanner that run automatically as in in static checks.

472
00:50:08,630 --> 00:50:18,150
So providing that feedback path, that feedback path to an agent is very important because it's how it learns about the world you've put it in.

473
00:50:18,150 --> 00:50:22,710
In terms of regressions, I'm afraid the answer there is going to be testing as it always is.

474
00:50:22,710 --> 00:50:30,069
Better development practices help as well though, and that includes better development practices for the AI.

475
00:50:30,069 --> 00:50:31,990
So any static checks you can do will help.

476
00:50:31,990 --> 00:50:34,069
Turning on all of your compiler warnings will help.

477
00:50:34,069 --> 00:50:38,630
and also anything you can do to help it understand the real context.

478
00:50:38,630 --> 00:50:44,069
So there's another interesting we talked about DBG.

479
00:50:44,069 --> 00:50:56,390
There's another interesting project called LDB which I think is LLM debugger and that's written about in a paper from the University of California San Diego called debug like a human

480
00:50:56,390 --> 00:51:04,390
and the subtitle there is a large language model debugger via verifying runtime execution step by step and they showed something

481
00:51:04,400 --> 00:51:13,430
actually really interesting which was that they gave an LLM that was being used as a coding agent the ability to step through code it had just generated

482
00:51:13,440 --> 00:51:19,910
and look and see if it did what it expected or if it had violated invariance that it expected to be there.

483
00:51:19,920 --> 00:51:30,630
And what they've shown is that actually giving a coding agent better insight into how the code they wrote behaves dynamically can make them smarter.

484
00:51:30,630 --> 00:51:44,549
So, I think there's a whole world here again in providing better kinds of context and better kinds of ground truth into an AI system because ultimately if you get that right, the AI become even smarter than they already are.

485
00:51:44,549 --> 00:51:46,790
They're already very good at coding.

486
00:51:46,790 --> 00:51:51,990
But if if you can point them in the right direction and give them the things they really need to know,

487
00:51:51,990 --> 00:52:04,069
actually you can unlock more of that capability and you can be using their intelligence for the right things which is writing your code instead of the wrong things which is puzzling through gaps in the data that you could easily get for them.

488
00:52:04,069 --> 00:52:15,990
The last thing masking the real root cause and actually I think this applies for regressions and security vulnerabilities as well is I'm afraid people don't usually like it but code review you still got to do it.

489
00:52:16,000 --> 00:52:20,069
You've still got somebody maybe with AI assistants as well,

490
00:52:20,069 --> 00:52:26,870
but ultimately somebody's still got to check that the tests don't pass now simply because the LLM deleted all of them

491
00:52:26,870 --> 00:52:35,910
or the LLM didn't put in an obvious backdoor into the system in the interest of making something else it thought you wanted possible.

492
00:52:35,910 --> 00:52:41,910
So I think there's got to be for the foreseeable future something that looks like our modern software development life cycle.

493
00:52:41,910 --> 00:52:44,390
maybe AI assisted but humans in the loop.

494
00:52:44,390 --> 00:52:53,109
Humans ultimately responsible for making sure this stuff is right and that the right code is written to match the the end users requirements.

495
00:52:53,119 --> 00:52:57,270
I think that's great. I think that's a very valid point.

496
00:52:57,270 --> 00:53:04,710
How do you trust an output and verify have some sort of a human in the loop to check the validity of the output also where possible?

497
00:53:04,710 --> 00:53:06,390
Yeah, I think that's great.

498
00:53:06,390 --> 00:53:08,549
I think that brings us to the end of our show.

499
00:53:08,549 --> 00:53:18,309
So it's been a fascinating conversation where we went right from treating the debugger as an assistant tooling to also looking at it being autonomous.

500
00:53:18,309 --> 00:53:20,309
So thank you so much for coming on the show Mark.

501
00:53:20,319 --> 00:53:23,030
It's been great having you. Thank you very much.

502
00:53:23,030 --> 00:53:26,069
It's been great to be here and very fun to talk about my favorite subjects.

